.TH IOMMU 3
.SH NAME
iommu \- DMA remapping
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
bind -a #Q /dev

/dev/iommu/ctl
/dev/iommu/clone
/dev/iommu/faults
/dev/iommu/\fIn\fP/ctl
/dev/iommu/\fIn\fP/devices
/dev/iommu/\fIn\fP/map
/dev/iommu/\fIn\fP/mem
/dev/iommu/\fIn\fP/faults
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Iommu
confines devices' DMA to address spaces called domains.  Reading
.B ctl
gives the backend (Intel VT-d, AMD-Vi, or on RISC-V the RISC-V IOMMU)
and its remapping units; writing
.B enable
turns translation on with every function the IOMMU covers in the host
domain, where DMA addresses are host physical addresses as before.
Functions that appear later (VFs made through
.BR #$/pci/ \fIfunction\fP /sriov )
join the host domain.
.PP
Opening
.B clone
(or writing
.B new
to
.BR ctl )
makes a domain.  Its
.B ctl
takes
.BI attach " 0000:bb:dd.f" \fR,
.BI detach " 0000:bb:dd.f" \fR,
.BI mem " iova bytes"
(kernel memory for the domain, mapped read-write at
.IR iova ,
read and written through
.B mem
at offset = IOVA),
.BI unmap " iova bytes"
and
.BR kill .
A function attached to a domain reaches by DMA only what the domain
maps; detached, it is back in the host domain.
.B map
lists what is mapped, and for
.B mem
the host physical address behind it.
.B faults
lists the DMA faults recorded: sequence, requester, read or write,
address and reason \(em in all, or for the domain's functions.
.PP
.IR vm (3)
makes domains from the kernel for assigned functions; they appear here
like any other.  When a machine ends, each function it had is moved
straight from its domain \(em never through the host domain \(em into
the
.IR "blocking domain" ,
made the first time it is needed: a numbered domain like the others
that maps nothing, so DMA the function still has queued, or starts
after, faults.  Its
.B devices
lists the functions released into it.  It refuses
.B kill
and
.BR mem .
A function leaves it when
.B attach
names it in another domain (taken straight from the blocking one) or
.B detach
gives it back to the host domain, which is how the host claims it;
.IR vm (3)'s
.B assign
takes it the same way.
.PP
Moving a function between domains is done by repointing its entry
(VT-d's context entry, AMD-Vi's device table entry); while the entry
changes it refuses DMA \(em VT-d's not present, AMD-Vi's valid with no
read or write permission \(em rather than passing it through.
.SH SOURCE
.B /os/inferno/port/deviommu.c
.br
.B /os/inferno/port/iommu.h
.br
.B /os/inferno/pc/vtd.c
.br
.B /os/inferno/pc/amdvi.c
.br
.B /os/inferno/pc/dmar.h
.br
.B /os/inferno/virt/rviommu.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR vm (3),
.IR pci (10)
.SH BUGS
VT-d: legacy mode, global invalidation after every change (queued
where the unit has it, through the registers where not).  Interrupt
remapping is on from boot where DMAR and every unit have it; requester
IDs are checked, an I/O APIC's only where DMAR names it with a one-step
path.  AMD-Vi: the
first IOMMU in IVRS only, no interrupt remapping.  The RISC-V IOMMU: no
MSI page table, so a device's messages are translated like its writes.
Tested on QEMU's intel-iommu, amd-iommu (with
.B scripts/qemu-patches/0005 )
and riscv-iommu-pci.  At most 32 domains and 64 functions in each.
